<h1>Bio</h1>
<p class="hero__lede">
  Where I have worked and studied, newest first, with a bit about what each phase
  was actually like.
</p>

<section class="phase">
  <h2>2021 to now</h2>
  <p class="phase__where">
    Software engineering researcher, <a href="https://www.huawei.com/ca/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Huawei Canada</a>,
    Centre for Software Excellence. Montreal, Quebec.
  </p>
  <p>
    I research how software gets built when a large part of it is written by, or
    delegated to, foundation models. That splits roughly into two halves: AI for
    software engineering, and software engineering for AI systems. In practice
    the two are hard to separate, because the tools we study are also the tools
    we build with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of my published work here has been about making these systems legible.
    <a href="/publications/">Watson</a> is a cognitive observability framework
    for the reasoning of LLM-powered agents, which came out of a fairly simple
    frustration: when an agent goes wrong, the trace tells you what it did and
    almost nothing about why. More recently I have been working on
    specification-driven development, the idea that the durable artifact is the
    stated intent rather than the generated code, and on labeling architectural
    reasoning in code models at a scale that does not require a human reading
    every diff.
  </p>
  <p>
    I joined from Kingston and eventually moved to Montreal. The work is
    industrial research, which means papers and prototypes both, and the ratio
    between them shifts month to month. I like that more than I expected to.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="phase">
  <h2>2020 to 2022</h2>
  <p class="phase__where">
    M.Sc. in Computing, <a href="https://www.queensu.ca/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Queen's University</a>,
    Software Analysis &amp; Intelligence Lab. Kingston, Ontario.
  </p>
  <p>
    I did my master's at SAIL with Ahmed E. Hassan, and most of my time went
    into dependency management bots. The
    <a href="/publications/">thesis</a> asks two questions about them: what do
    they actually cost the projects that adopt them, and can the crowd of other
    projects already running the same upgrade tell you whether yours is safe. The
    Greenkeeper study is the first half, the Dependabot compatibility score study
    is the second.
  </p>
  <p>
    Empirical software engineering was a real adjustment after a few years of
    shipping product. The unit of work stops being a feature and becomes a
    claim you have to defend, and a lot of the effort goes into the parts nobody
    reads: mining the data, deciding what counts as a signal, convincing yourself
    the result is not an artifact of how you sampled. Filipe Cogo walked me
    through most of that.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was also head TA for CISC221, Computer Architecture, for two years.
    Teaching a course you did not take yourself is a good way to find out how
    shallow your understanding of something is.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="phase">
  <h2>2018 to 2020</h2>
  <p class="phase__where">
    Software developer, <a href="https://eigen.io/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Eigen Innovations</a>.
    Fredericton, New Brunswick.
  </p>
  <p>
    I started at Eigen as a front-end co-op student and came back full time after
    graduating. Eigen builds industrial machine vision for manufacturing, so the
    software sits between cameras on a factory floor and the people who need to
    know when a part came out wrong. That gave me an unusually wide surface for a
    first real job: Vue in the browser, Django and Postgres behind it, an
    Electron app for the plant floor, and C and C++ where the cameras were.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the phase where I learned what production actually means. The
    customer's line does not stop because your abstraction was elegant, and a
    deployment you cannot debug over a slow connection to a plant in another
    province is not a deployment. I still think of it as the most
    formative two years I have had.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="phase">
  <h2>2014 to 2019</h2>
  <p class="phase__where">
    B.Sc. in Software Engineering, co-operative education program,
    <a href="https://www.unb.ca/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">University of New Brunswick</a>.
    Fredericton, New Brunswick.
  </p>
  <p>
    Software engineering at UNB is a joint degree between computer science and
    electrical and computer engineering, which in practice meant alternating
    between compilers and circuits. I took the co-op stream, so the five years
    were split between terms on campus and terms working.
  </p>
  <p>
    The placements went, in order: IT support and then development at
    <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/brunswick-news-inc-/?originalSubdomain=ca" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Brunswick News</a>,
    quality assurance at <a href="https://www.siemens.com/ca/en.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Siemens Canada</a>,
    a research assistantship in UNB's
    <a href="https://hcilab.github.io/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Human-Computer Interaction Lab</a>
    building Android and Bluetooth LE prototypes, and finally Eigen. Four
    very different kinds of software organization before I had a degree, which is
    the whole argument for co-op.
  </p>
  <p>
    The HCI lab term is the one that changed the trajectory. It was the first
    time I saw research as something a person could do as a job rather than
    something that happened in papers, and it is a straight line from there to
    SAIL and then to where I am now.
  </p>
</section>
